{#
  Container `resources:` requests and limits — the four-quantity block ~100 container specs
  across the k8s roles share, differing only in the values and in whether `limits` or
  `requests` was typed first (69 templates one way, 23 the other, for no reason beyond which
  sibling each role was copied from).

  NOT the same key as two other `resources:` in this tree, and this macro is for neither:
  a PersistentVolumeClaim's `spec.resources.requests.storage` (see pvc.yml.j2), and an RBAC
  Role's `rules[].resources`, which is a list of API resource names. Both sit at a different
  depth and neither has cpu/memory under it.

  Indentation is fixed at 10 spaces, the container-list depth in a Deployment, DaemonSet,
  StatefulSet or CronJob pod template here — 106 of the fleet's 108 container blocks. The two
  nested deeper (a CronJob's `jobTemplate`) stay inline, for the same reason the securityContext
  macro leaves its two: a macro that can emit at two depths can emit at the wrong one.

  NOT FOR image-builder OR seed-volume. Both are in validate_k8s_manifests.SKIP_ROLES and
  cannot be rendered standalone, so test_namespace_resource_defaults.py's
  `test_the_roles_outside_the_render_walk_are_accounted_for` checks their pod specs by grepping
  raw template text for `requests:` and `limits:`. It asserts the two appear together, because
  a request with no limit takes the namespace default and is REFUSED at admission if the
  request is higher — surfacing hours later as a failed image build. Behind this macro that
  check compares False to False and passes on nothing. build-job.yaml.j2 was converted during
  this macro's own rollout and caught exactly that way; the guard now asserts its own coverage.

  QUANTITIES ARE EMITTED QUOTED. Every value in this family is a Kubernetes quantity string —
  `250m`, `128Mi`, `1` — so quoting is a no-op to the parsed YAML while making the emitted form
  uniform. That is checked, not assumed: rendering the fleet before and after this macro landed
  produced identical parsed YAML for all 281 manifests.

  Arguments:
    cpu_limit, mem_limit, cpu_request, mem_request
        the four quantities, as values not as pre-quoted text.
    extra   an extended-resource NAME (jellyfin's and tdarr's `*_k8s_dri_resource`) to request
            and limit at 1. Kubernetes REJECTS an extended resource whose request and limit
            differ, so it is deliberately one argument emitting both rather than two that could
            drift apart.
#}
{% macro container_resources(cpu_limit, mem_limit, cpu_request, mem_request, extra=none) %}
          resources:
            limits:
              cpu: "{{ cpu_limit }}"
              memory: "{{ mem_limit }}"
{% if extra %}
              {{ extra }}: 1
{% endif %}
            requests:
              cpu: "{{ cpu_request }}"
              memory: "{{ mem_request }}"
{% if extra %}
              {{ extra }}: 1
{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
